  "question": {
    "text": "An electron in a hydrogen atom makes a transition from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and emits a photon. This photon strikes a doubly ionized lithium atom which was already in an excited state and completely removes the orbiting electron. The least quantum number for the excited state of the lithium-ion for the process is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "PrhRD",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "pscrs",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "fGJlA",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "euBFA",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let us assume that the electron in the&nbsp;lithium-ion is knocked off from the&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>th</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;energy level, then</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>&#8722;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mfrac><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>Li</mi></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><msup><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mfenced><mn>3</mn></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\" separators=\"|\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>12</mn></msqrt><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p>Hence the least quantum number must be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
